[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step checklist** that walks you from a working provider-style URL to a custom style that actually draws.  
It follows the “MapLibre Source Wiring” skill, so you can see exactly which part of the wiring is most likely broken when *nothing* appears even though the console is clean.

---

## 1️⃣ Start with the provider’s source definition

The provider’s style URL already contains a **source object** that works. Grab that object (or the TileJSON URL it points at) and copy it into your own style.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json"   // <-- TileJSON endpoint
    }
  },
  "layers": []   // you’ll add your own layers later
}
```

**Why?**  
* `url` → TileJSON gives MapLibre the tile template, min/max-zoom, bounds, and – most importantly – the list of **source-layer names** (`vector_layers`). If you instead hand-write a `tiles` array you lose that metadata and you’ll have to supply `minzoom`/`maxzoom` yourself.

---

## 2️⃣ Verify the source-layer names

Open the TileJSON in a browser (or fetch it with `curl`) and look at the `vector_layers` array:

```json
{
  "vector_layers": [
    { "id": "transportation", "minzoom": 5, "maxzoom": 14, "fields": { ... } },
    { "id": "water",          "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14, "fields": { ... } },
    { "id": "landuse",        "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14, "fields": { ... } }
    // …
  ]
}
```

**Take note of the exact `id` strings** – they are case-sensitive and must be used verbatim in every style layer that draws from this source.

> **Common pitfall:** using `"transportation"` when the TileJSON actually calls the layer `"transportation_roads"` (or vice-versa). The map stays silent – no error, no output.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a test layer that you know exists

Create the simplest possible layer that references one of the source-layers you just inspected. If this renders, the wiring is correct; if it still doesn’t, the problem is elsewhere (CORS, tileSize, etc.).

```json
{
  "id": "test-roads",
  "type": "line",
  "source": "basemap",
  "source-layer": "transportation",   // <-- must match TileJSON exactly
  "paint": {
    "line-color": "#ff0000",
    "line-width": 2
  }
}
```

Add it with `map.addLayer()` **before** any symbol (label) layers, or insert it before the first symbol layer:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer in the existing style
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert your layer right before it
map.addLayer(testRoads, firstSymbol);
```

If you see a thin red line where roads should be, the source is wired correctly.

---

## 4️⃣ If the test layer is still invisible, check these “silent-fail” culprits

| Symptom | What to look for | Fix |
|---------|------------------|-----|
| **All network requests 200 but nothing draws** | `source-layer` typo or wrong schema (e.g., using OpenMapTiles names on a Shortbread tileset) | Use the exact `id` from `vector_layers`. |
| **Requests 404 / 403** | Wrong tile URL (maybe you used a raster template for a vector source) | Use the TileJSON `url` or correct `{z}/{x}/{y}` template. |
| **CORS error in console** | `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` missing on the tile server | Enable CORS on the server or use a CDN that adds it. |
| **Labels disappear after you add your layer** | You added the layer *after* the label (`symbol`) layers, covering them | Insert your layer before the first symbol layer (see step 3). |
| **Map looks blurry / zoomed one level off** | Raster source with default `tileSize: 512` but tiles are 256px | Set `tileSize: 256` in the source definition. |
| **Icons missing** | No `sprite` URL in the style root, or sprite URL 404s | Add `"sprite": "https://tiles.example.com/sprite"` (or your own). |
| **Text in wrong font** | `glyphs` URL missing or failing | Add `"glyphs": "https://tiles.example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"`. |

---

## 5️⃣ Wire the rest of your custom layers

Now that the source is proven to work, copy the pattern from the test layer for each of your own layers:

```json
{
  "id": "my-water-polys",
  "type": "fill",
  "source": "basemap",
  "source-layer": "water",          // exact name from TileJSON
  "paint": {
    "fill-color": "#a0c8f0",
    "fill-opacity": 0.6
  }
}
```

**Layer order tip:**  
If you have many layers, keep a small helper that always inserts *before* the first symbol layer:

```js
function addBelowLabels(layer) {
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
  map.addLayer(layer, firstSymbol);
}
```

---

## 6️⃣ Feature-state sanity check (optional)

If you plan to use `setFeatureState` on this source, make sure the source provides **feature IDs**. For a vector source you usually need `promoteId`:

```json
{
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json",
  "promoteId": { "transportation": "osm_id" }   // property inside each feature
}
```

Without `promoteId` (or `generateId: true`), `setFeatureState` silently does nothing.

---

## 7️⃣ Quick “debug-once-and-done” script

Paste this into the browser console after the map has loaded. It will:

1. List all source-layers the source actually provides.  
2. Show the first symbol layer ID (where you should insert your layers).  
3. Verify CORS on the tile endpoint.

```js
(async () => {
  const src = map.getSource('basemap');
  if (!src) return console.error('Source "basemap" not found');

  // 1️⃣ Get TileJSON (only works if source was defined with `url`)
  const tilejsonUrl = src._options?.url;
  if (tilejsonUrl) {
    const resp = await fetch(tilejsonUrl);
    const tj = await resp.json();
    console.log('🗺️  TileJSON vector_layers:', tj.vector_layers?.map(l => l.id));
  } else {
    console.warn('Source defined with `tiles` array – no TileJSON to inspect');
  }

  // 2️⃣ First symbol layer
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  console.log('🔤 First symbol layer ID:', firstSymbol?.id);

  // 3️⃣ CORS test (fetch a single tile)
  const testTile = `https://tiles.example.com/0/0/0.pbf`; // adjust for your source
  try {
    const r = await fetch(testTile, { mode: 'cors' });
    console.log('✅ Tile fetch status:', r.status, r.headers.get('Access-Control-Allow-Origin'));
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('❌ Tile fetch failed (CORS?)', e);
  }
})();
```

If the console prints the list of `vector_layers` and the first symbol ID, you now have the exact strings you need for `source-layer` and layer insertion.

---

## 📋 TL;DR Checklist

| ✅ Done? | Action |
|---------|--------|
| **Source definition** | Use `url` → TileJSON (or `tiles` + explicit `minzoom`/`maxzoom`). |
| **Source-layer names** | Pull exact `id`s from `vector_layers` in the TileJSON. |
| **Test layer** | Add a minimal line/fill layer referencing a known `source-layer`. |
| **Layer order** | Insert before the first `symbol` layer (or use `addLayer(layer, firstSymbolId)`). |
| **CORS** | Verify the tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. |
| **Raster tileSize** | If using raster tiles, set `tileSize: 256` for classic 256-px tiles. |
| **Glyphs & sprite** | Provide working `glyphs` and `sprite` URLs if you need text or icons. |
| **Feature state** | Add `promoteId` if you’ll use `setFeatureState`. |

Follow the list in order; the first thing that fails is almost always the cause of a completely blank custom style.

---

### 🎉 You should now see your custom layers rendered on top of the provider’s basemap.  
If you still get a blank map after confirming every line above, drop the exact style JSON (or a minimal reproducible snippet) into a new question and we can dig deeper. Happy styling!

### TL;DR  
Add (or move) your data layer **before** the first *symbol* layer in the style – the layers that draw street-name labels, POI icons, etc.  
The easiest way is to call `map.addLayer(yourLayer, firstSymbolId)` (or `map.moveLayer(yourLayer.id, firstSymbolId)`).  
If you don’t know the exact symbol-layer id, discover it at runtime by scanning the style’s layer list for the first layer whose `type` is `"symbol"`.

---

## Why the order matters

MapLibre (just like Mapbox GL JS) renders layers **bottom-to-top** in the order they appear in the style JSON.  
When you call

```js
map.addLayer(myLayer);   // no second argument
```

the layer is appended to the end of the style, i.e. **above everything else**.  
If the style already contains a bunch of `symbol` layers for street names, road shields, POI icons, etc., your new layer will sit on top of them and hide the labels.

---

## The canonical fix: insert *below* the first symbol layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Find the id of the first symbol layer in the current style
function getFirstSymbolLayerId() {
  const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
  for (const layer of layers) {
    if (layer.type === 'symbol') {
      return layer.id;          // <-- this is the insertion point
    }
  }
  // If the style has no symbol layers (unlikely for a basemap) fall back to null
  return null;
}

// 2️⃣  Add your data layer *before* that symbol layer
const myLayer = {
  id: 'my-parcels',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',          // whatever source you created
  paint: {
    'fill-color': '#ff6600',
    'fill-opacity': 0.4
  }
};

const firstSymbolId = getFirstSymbolLayerId();

if (firstSymbolId) {
  // Insert below the first symbol layer
  map.addLayer(myLayer, firstSymbolId);
} else {
  // No symbol layers – just add it (nothing to hide)
  map.addLayer(myLayer);
}
```

### What the second argument does
`map.addLayer(layer, beforeId)` tells MapLibre: *“Insert `layer` right **before** the layer whose id is `beforeId`.”*  
Because the style is rendered bottom-to-top, everything that appears **after** `beforeId` (including all the street-name symbols) will be drawn **on top of** your layer.

---

## If the layer is already on the map

If you added the layer earlier without the `beforeId` argument, you can reposition it later:

```js
if (firstSymbolId) {
  map.moveLayer('my-parcels', firstSymbolId); // move existing layer
}
```

`moveLayer(id, beforeId)` works the same way as `addLayer` – it reorders the layer without needing to remove and re-add it.

---

## A few practical tips & gotchas

| Situation | What to do |
|-----------|------------|
| **You have multiple custom layers** and want them all *under* the labels but *above* the basemap raster/vector layers. | Insert each of them **before** the first symbol layer (the same `firstSymbolId`). Their relative order among themselves will stay as you add them. |
| **Your style uses a custom “label” layer that isn’t a `symbol`** (e.g., a `text` layer from a third-party style). | Look for the first layer whose `type` is `"symbol"` **or** whose `layout['text-field']` is defined. The same scanning logic works; just adjust the predicate. |
| **You’re using a style that already injects custom layers** (e.g., a third-party basemap that adds its own data layers). | Run the `getFirstSymbolLayerId()` *after* the style has finished loading (`map.on('style.load', …)`). That guarantees you see all built-in layers. |
| **You want your layer *above* some symbols but *below* others** (e.g., you want road labels to stay on top but hide POI labels). | Find the specific symbol layer you want to stay on top of (e.g., `road-label`) and insert before that one. You can also insert after a particular layer by passing the id of the layer you want to be **below** (`map.addLayer(myLayer, afterId)` where `afterId` is the id of the layer you want to stay underneath). |
| **You’re using a raster source for the basemap** (no `symbol` layers). | Raster basemaps usually have *no* label layers; the labels come from a separate vector source. In that case you’ll need to locate the first vector `symbol` layer (often something like `road-label`, `place-label`, etc.) and insert before it. |
| **You’re using a style that already has a `waterway-label` layer but you want your data under it**. | Use `map.moveLayer('my-parcels', 'waterway-label')`. The layer will be placed just below `waterway-label`. |

---

## Full example – from map creation to correct ordering

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – keep labels on top</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any vector style with labels
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Wait until the style (and its layers) are fully loaded
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source
    map.addSource('parcels', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://example.com/parcels.geojson',
      promoteId: 'parcel_id'   // optional – needed for feature-state
    });

    // 2️⃣ Find the first symbol layer (the label layer)
    const firstSymbolId = (() => {
      const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
      for (const l of layers) {
        if (l.type === 'symbol') return l.id;
      }
      return null;
    })();

    // 3️⃣ Define the visual layer
    const parcelLayer = {
      id: 'parcels-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'parcels',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#ff6600',
        'fill-opacity': 0.3
      }
    };

    // 4️⃣ Insert it before the first label layer
    if (firstSymbolId) {
      map.addLayer(parcelLayer, firstSymbolId);
    } else {
      // No symbol layers – just add it (nothing to hide)
      map.addLayer(parcelLayer);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Running the snippet above will render the parcel polygons **under** all street-name and POI labels, preserving the map’s readability.

---

## Recap checklist

1. **Wait for the style to load** (`style.load` event) before you query layer order.  
2. **Find the first `symbol` layer** (or the specific label layer you care about).  
3. **Add or move your custom layer** with the second argument (`beforeId`) so it sits *below* that symbol layer.  
4. **Verify** – open the browser dev-tools → Network → see that tile requests succeed, and visually confirm that labels are still visible.

That’s all you need to keep your data layer from swallowing the street names! 🎉

Below is a **complete, minimal checklist** for getting the classic OSM raster tiles to appear in a MapLibre map.  
Everything you have already written is correct, but a couple of required pieces are easy to forget:

| What you have | What is still needed | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------------|----------------|
| `type: 'raster'`<br>`tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png']` | **`tileSize: 256`** | The raster source defaults to 512 px tiles. OSM serves 256 px tiles, so without `tileSize: 256` the imagery will be rendered at the wrong zoom (it looks “zoomed out” and the basemap labels will be mis-aligned). |
| – | **A raster layer that points at the source** | A source on its own does nothing; you must add a style layer (`type: 'raster'`) that uses that source. |
| – | **Attribution** (optional but required by OSM’s licence) | OSM tiles must be credited. If you omit it the map will still render, but you’ll be violating the tile-use policy. |
| – | **(Optional) `minzoom` / `maxzoom`** | OSM tiles exist from zoom 0-19. Supplying the range prevents MapLibre from requesting zoom levels that don’t exist (it will just get empty tiles). |
| – | **(Optional) Layer order** | If you add the raster layer after a vector-style basemap, it will sit **on top** and hide everything else (including labels). Insert it **below the first `symbol` layer** (or before any label layers) unless you really want it to be the topmost layer. |
| – | **CORS** | OSM already sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so no extra work is needed. |

---

## Full, ready-to-copy example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>OSM raster source in MapLibre</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // a very small “empty” style – we’ll inject the OSM raster layer later
      sources: {},
      layers: []
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the raster source
    map.addSource('osm-raster', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
      tileSize: 256,               // ← required for OSM tiles
      minzoom: 0,
      maxzoom: 19,
      attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that uses the source
    // Insert it *below* the first symbol layer (labels) if you have any other layers.
    // Since our style is empty we can just add it at the top.
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'osm-basemap',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'osm-raster',
      paint: {
        // optional: fade in tiles (default 300 ms)
        'raster-fade-duration': 0
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (If you later add vector layers) keep the raster below labels:
    // const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
    // if (firstSymbol) {
    //   map.moveLayer('osm-basemap', firstSymbol);
    // }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Code line | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Aligns MapLibre’s internal tile grid with OSM’s 256 px tiles. |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Prevents MapLibre from requesting zoom 20+ (which OSM does not provide). |
| `attribution` | Satisfies OSM’s licence requirement. |
| `addLayer({type:'raster', source:'osm-raster'})` | Actually draws the tiles. |
| `moveLayer` (commented) | Shows the pattern for inserting the raster **below** the first label (`symbol`) layer if you later add vector data. |

---

## Quick “debug checklist” if you still see a blank map

1. **Network tab** – Do you see successful `200` responses for `…/0/0/0.png` etc.?  
   *If you see CORS errors, the server isn’t sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. OSM does, so the problem is likely a typo in the URL.*

2. **Console** – Any warnings about missing `source-layer`?  
   *Raster sources don’t need `source-layer`; this warning would indicate you accidentally added a vector layer pointing at the raster source.*

3. **Zoom level** – Does the map look “zoomed out” (tiles look blurry or duplicated)?  
   *If yes, you probably omitted `tileSize: 256`.*

4. **Layer order** – Are other layers (e.g., vector labels) hidden?  
   *If yes, move the raster layer below the first `symbol` layer (`map.moveLayer('osm-basemap', firstSymbolId)`).*

5. **Attribution** – Not required for rendering, but check the bottom-right corner to confirm the OSM credit appears (good practice).

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.addSource('osm-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,               // **required**
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-raster'
});
```

Add the layer **before** any label (`symbol`) layers if you want the OSM labels to stay visible. With those three pieces (`tileSize`, a raster layer, and attribution) the OSM tiles will render correctly. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:**  
No. A MapLibre *style* (the JSON you found) is **not** a data source and cannot be added with `map.addSource()`. A style is a complete description of *how* to draw a map – it contains a list of sources, a list of layers, sprite & glyph URLs, etc. To reuse just one of its layers you have to **extract** the pieces you need (the source definition + the layer definition) and add those to your own map manually.

Below is a step-by-step guide that shows exactly what to copy, what to adapt, and the common pitfalls that make a “layer-works-in-the-original-style-but-nothing-shows-here” problem look like a CORS or zoom-range issue.

---

## 1. Understand the three parts you’ll be pulling out

| Part | What it is | Where it lives in the style JSON |
|------|------------|----------------------------------|
| **Source definition** | Describes *where* the tiles/GeoJSON live (type, URL, tiles array, minzoom/maxzoom, etc.) | `style.sources.<sourceId>` |
| **Layer definition** | Describes *how* to draw (type, paint, layout, `source`, `source-layer`, filter, etc.) | `style.layers[i]` |
| **Sprite / Glyph URLs** | Needed only if the layer you are copying uses symbols (icons or text). | `style.sprite`, `style.glyphs` |

A **background layer** (`"type": "background"`) does **not** need a source at all – you can copy the whole layer object directly. Most “background” layers in public styles are actually raster or vector tile layers that fill the map, so they *do* need a source.

---

## 2. Pull the source definition into your map

### 2.1. If the source uses a TileJSON endpoint (`url`)

```js
// Example from the remote style
// "sources": {
//   "basemap": {
//     "type": "vector",
//     "url": "https://example.com/tileset.json"
//   }
// }

map.addSource('basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://example.com/tileset.json'   // keep exactly the same URL
});
```

*Why `url` is preferred*: MapLibre will read the TileJSON, get the correct `tiles` template, zoom range, and the list of `vector_layers`. No need to manually copy `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, `bounds`, etc.

### 2.2. If the source only provides a tile-template (`tiles` array)

```js
// "sources": {
//   "satellite": {
//     "type": "raster",
//     "tiles": ["https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
//     "tileSize": 256,          // <-- very important for classic 256-px tiles
//     "minzoom": 0,
//     "maxzoom": 18
//   }
// }

map.addSource('satellite', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,          // omit only if the server really serves 512-px tiles
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 18
});
```

> **Common gotcha** – forgetting `tileSize: 256` on a classic OSM-style raster source makes the imagery appear one zoom level off (blurred, shifted, or missing at high zooms) with no console error.

### 2.3. If the source is GeoJSON

```js
map.addSource('my-data', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://mydomain.com/data.geojson',
  promoteId: 'my_id'   // only needed if you plan to use feature-state later
});
```

---

## 3. Copy the layer definition

Take the layer object you want from the remote style and paste it into your own `addLayer` call. **Only change the `id` (if you need a unique one) and the `source` name** if you renamed the source in step 2.

```js
// Example layer from the remote style
// {
//   "id": "land",
//   "type": "fill",
//   "source": "basemap",
//   "source-layer": "landcover",
//   "paint": { "fill-color": "#f0f0f0" }
// }

map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-land',               // any unique id you like
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'basemap',           // must match the source you added above
  'source-layer': 'landcover',// exact name from the TileJSON's vector_layers
  paint: { 'fill-color': '#f0f0f0' }
});
```

### 3.1. `source-layer` sanity check

*If the layer is vector-based* you **must** supply the correct `source-layer`. The name must match **exactly** what the TileJSON reports in its `vector_layers` array. A typo or a layer name from a different schema results in a silent “nothing draws” situation.

**How to verify:**  
Open the TileJSON URL in a browser (or fetch it with `curl`). Look for:

```json
"vector_layers": [
  { "id": "landcover", "description": "...", "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14 },
  ...
]
```

If the `vector_layers` field is missing (some older TileJSONs), consult the provider’s documentation or use a tool like `tile-join --list` (tippecanoe) to inspect the schema.

### 3.2. Layer order – don’t hide labels

Layers are drawn **bottom-to-top** in the order they appear in the style. If you call `map.addLayer()` without a second argument, the new layer goes on top of everything, which often hides street-name symbols.

**Best practice:** Insert the layer *just below* the first symbol layer:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer (usually a label layer)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert your layer right before it
map.addLayer(myLayerObject, firstSymbol);
```

If you’re adding a **background** layer (`type: "background"`), you can simply add it first (or set `layout: { "visibility": "none" }` and toggle later).

---

## 4. Sprite & glyph URLs – only needed for symbol layers

If the layer you copied uses `icon-image`, `text-field`, or any other symbol-related paint/layout properties, you also need the **same** `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs that the original style used. They are *style-root* properties, not source properties, so you must set them on the map **before** adding the layer:

```js
map.setStyle({
  version: 8,
  sources: {},          // you’ll add your own sources later
  layers: [],           // start empty
  sprite: 'https://example.com/sprites/basic',
  glyphs: 'https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf'
});
```

Or, if you already have a style loaded, you can just set the properties directly:

```js
map.setSprite('https://example.com/sprites/basic');
map.setGlyphs('https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf');
```

> **Missing sprite** → console warnings like `Image "airport-15" could not be loaded`.  
> **Missing glyphs** → text appears in the browser’s default font (GL JS ≥ 5.11) or disappears (older versions).

---

## 5. CORS – the silent blocker

If any of the URLs you added (tiles, sprite, glyphs, GeoJSON) are on a different origin, the server **must** send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). Otherwise the browser blocks the request and the map stays blank with no error in the MapLibre console (you’ll see a CORS error in the Network tab).

**Quick test:** Open the tile URL directly in a new tab. If you get a “blocked by CORS policy” message in the console, you need to configure the server or use a CDN that adds the header.

---

## 6. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Reuse a remote background layer</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add our own sources below
      layers: [],           // start empty
      sprite: 'https://example.com/sprites/basic',
      glyphs: 'https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf'
    },
    center: [-122.44, 37.76],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the remote vector source (TileJSON endpoint)
    map.addSource('remote-basemap', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://example.com/tileset.json'   // <-- copy from the remote style
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add the layer you want to reuse
    const remoteLandLayer = {
      id: 'remote-land',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'remote-basemap',
      'source-layer': 'landcover',   // must match TileJSON's vector_layers
      paint: { 'fill-color': '#e0e0e0' }
    };

    // Insert it below the first symbol layer of your own style (if any)
    const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
    map.addLayer(remoteLandLayer, firstSymbol);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace the URLs with the ones from the style you found, double-check the `source-layer` name, and you’ll see the remote background rendered **under** your own basemap layers.

---

## 7. TL;DR checklist

| ✅ What you need to do | ❌ What you cannot do |
|------------------------|----------------------|
| Copy the **source** object (`type`, `url` or `tiles`, `tileSize`, etc.) into `map.addSource()` | Pass the whole style JSON to `addSource()` – a style is not a source |
| Copy the **layer** object, adjust `id` and `source` (and keep the exact `source-layer` name) | Use a layer that references a source you never added – it will draw nothing |
| If the layer is a **symbol**, also copy the `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs to the map style | Assume the original style’s sprite/glyphs will magically work without setting them |
| Ensure **CORS** headers are present on every cross-origin request | Ignore CORS – the map will stay blank with no console error |
| Insert the new layer **before** the first `symbol` layer (or wherever you need it in the stack) | Add the layer last and watch your labels disappear |
| For **feature-state** usage, add `promoteId` (or integer `id`s) to the source | Call `setFeatureState` on a source that has no feature IDs – it silently fails |

---

### Bottom line

> **You cannot add a style JSON as a source.**  
> Extract the needed source definition(s) and layer definition(s), add those with `map.addSource()` and `map.addLayer()`, and make sure the ancillary assets (sprite, glyphs) and CORS headers are in place. Once those pieces line up, the remote background layer will render perfectly on top of (or beneath) your own bas

### TL;DR  
`setFeatureState` only works on **features that have an *id***.  
When your GeoJSON only carries the identifier inside `properties.parcel_id` you have to tell MapLibre to **promote that property to the feature id** when you add the source.  

```js
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: parcelsGeojson,
  promoteId: 'parcel_id'          // <-- this is the missing piece
});
```

After that the usual pattern works:

```js
// 1️⃣ paint expression that reacts to the state
map.addLayer({
  id: 'parcels-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',
  paint: {
    'fill-color': [
      'case',
      ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
      '#ff6600',          // hovered colour
      '#3388ff'           // default colour
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.5
  }
});

// 2️⃣ mouse handlers
let hoveredId = null;

map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', e => {
  if (!e.features.length) return;

  const feature = e.features[0];
  const id = feature.id;               // <-- comes from parcel_id thanks to promoteId

  if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
  }

  hoveredId = id;
  map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
});

map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});
```

If you skip `promoteId`, the features have **no id** (MapLibre generates none for GeoJSON).  
Calling `setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'12345'}, …)` silently does nothing, `getFeatureState` returns `{}` and the paint expression never sees the `hover` flag – exactly the behaviour you described.

---

## Why `promoteId` is the right fix (and why the other tricks don’t work)

| What you tried | What actually happens | Why it fails |
|----------------|----------------------|--------------|
| `setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'APN-1234'}, …)` | No error, no state change | The source has **no feature ids**. `id` is looked up in the source’s internal id table – it’s empty, so the call is a no-op. |
| `generateId: true` on the source | MapLibre creates numeric ids **by array index** | Those ids are *not* the business key (`parcel_id`). They change whenever the data array is reordered or filtered, so the state you set later will attach to the wrong feature (or disappear). |
| Adding an `id` property inside each GeoJSON feature | Works **only if the id is a top-level property** and is a number (or a string that can be cast to a number). | Your `parcel_id` lives inside `properties`, so MapLibre never sees it as the feature id. |
| No `promoteId` at all | Features have **no id** → `setFeatureState` is a silent no-op. | Exactly the symptom you see. |

`promoteId` tells MapLibre: *“Take the value of `properties.parcel_id` and treat it as the feature’s id.”*  
* It works for **GeoJSON** sources.  
* For **vector** sources you can also use `promoteId`, either a single property name (applies to all source-layers) or an object `{sourceLayer: propertyName}`.

---

## Checklist – make sure every piece is in place

1. **Source definition**  
   ```js
   map.addSource('parcels', {
     type: 'geojson',
     data: parcelsGeojson,
     promoteId: 'parcel_id'   // <-- required
   });
   ```

2. **Layer that uses the source** – no `source-layer` needed for GeoJSON.  
   Make sure the layer’s `paint` (or `layout`) uses a `feature-state` expression, e.g.:

   ```js
   'fill-color': [
     'case',
     ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
     '#ff6600',
     '#3388ff'
   ]
   ```

3. **Event handling** – call `setFeatureState` **after the source is added** (usually inside `map.on('load')` or after you add the source).  
   Use the feature’s `id` (which now comes from `parcel_id`) when you set or clear the state.

4. **Unique ids** – `parcel_id` must be unique across the whole GeoJSON collection. Duplicate ids will cause the state to be shared between features, which looks like “nothing happens”.

5. **Debugging tips**  

   * **Inspect a feature** in the `mousemove` handler: `console.log(e.features[0])`. You should see an `id` field equal to the parcel’s `parcel_id`.  
   * **Check the state** after you set it:  
     ```js
     console.log(map.getFeatureState({source:'parcels', id: feature.id}));
     // → { hover: true } (or whatever you set)
     ```
   * **Network tab** – there’s no network request for `setFeatureState`; it’s purely client-side. If you see nothing in the console, the most common cause is “no id”.

6. **CORS / loading** – not relevant for `setFeatureState`, but make sure the GeoJSON itself loads without CORS errors; otherwise the source never gets added and the handlers never fire.

---

## Full Minimal Example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Parcel hover with feature-state</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.45, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Example GeoJSON – replace with your real data
  const parcelsGeojson = {
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features: [
      {
        type: 'Feature',
        properties: { parcel_id: 'A001', name: 'Parcel A' },
        geometry: {
          type: 'Polygon',
          coordinates: [[
            [-122.45, 37.78],
            [-122.44, 37.78],
            [-122.44, 37.79],
            [-122.45, 37.79],
            [-122.45, 37.78]
          ]]
        }
      },
      // …more parcels
    ]
  };

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ add the source with promoteId
    map.addSource('parcels', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: parcelsGeojson,
      promoteId: 'parcel_id'
    });

    // 2️⃣ add a fill layer that reacts to the hover state
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'parcels-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'parcels',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': [
          'case',
          ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
          '#ff6600',
          '#3388ff'
        ],
        'fill-opacity': 0.5
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ mouse handlers
    let hoveredId = null;

    map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', e => {
      if (!e.features.length) return;
      const feature = e.features[0];
      const id = feature.id; // comes from parcel_id

      if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
      }
      hoveredId = id;
      map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
    });

    map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
      if (hoveredId !== null) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
        hoveredId = null;
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run the page, move the mouse over a parcel – it should turn orange. If it stays the default blue, open the console and check that `feature.id` is present; if it’s `undefined` you missed `promoteId`.

---

### Recap of the root cause

* **Missing `promoteId`** → features have no id → `setFeatureState` silently does nothing → paint expression never sees the state.  
* Adding `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` (or promoting a different property) gives each feature a stable id, after which `setFeatureState` works exactly as documented.

That’s all you need to get hover highlighting working on your parcel GeoJSON. Happy mapping!
